## Solution

We evaluate $\displaystyle I=\int_0^{\infty}\frac{\arctan^2(x)}{x^2}\,dx$.

**Step 1: Integration by parts.** Take $u=\arctan^2 x$ and $dv=x^{-2}\,dx$, so $du=\dfrac{2\arctan x}{1+x^2}\,dx$ and $v=-1/x$. Then

$$I=\left[-\frac{\arctan^2 x}{x}\right]_0^{\infty}+\int_0^{\infty}\frac{2\arctan x}{x(1+x^2)}\,dx.$$

The boundary term vanishes: as $x\to\infty$, $\arctan^2 x/x\to (\pi/2)^2/\infty\to 0$; as $x\to 0^+$, $\arctan x\sim x$, so $\arctan^2 x/x\sim x\to 0$. Hence

$$I=2\int_0^{\infty}\frac{\arctan x}{x(1+x^2)}\,dx.$$

**Step 2: Substitution $t=\arctan x$.** Then $x=\tan t$, $dx=\sec^2 t\,dt$, $1+x^2=\sec^2 t$, and $t:0\to\pi/2$. Thus

$$\frac{\arctan x}{x(1+x^2)}\,dx=\frac{t}{\tan t\,\sec^2 t}\,\sec^2 t\,dt=\frac{t\cos t}{\sin t}\,dt,$$

so

$$I=2\int_0^{\pi/2}\frac{t\cos t}{\sin t}\,dt.$$

**Step 3: Integration by parts again.** Let $u=t$, $dv=\dfrac{\cos t}{\sin t}\,dt$, so $v=\ln\sin t$. Then

$$\int_0^{\pi/2}\frac{t\cos t}{\sin t}\,dt=\Big[t\ln\sin t\Big]_0^{\pi/2}-\int_0^{\pi/2}\ln\sin t\,dt.$$

The boundary term vanishes: at $t=\pi/2$, $\ln\sin(\pi/2)=\ln 1=0$; at $t\to 0^+$, $t\ln\sin t\sim t\ln t\to 0$.

**Step 4: The classical integral.** Using the well-known identity

$$\int_0^{\pi/2}\ln\sin t\,dt=-\frac{\pi}{2}\ln 2$$

(which follows from $2\int_0^{\pi/2}\ln\sin t\,dt=\int_0^{\pi/2}\ln(\sin t\cos t)\,dt=\int_0^{\pi/2}\ln\!\big(\tfrac12\sin 2t\big)\,dt=-\tfrac{\pi}{2}\ln 2+\tfrac12\int_0^{\pi}\ln\sin u\,du$, giving $J=-\tfrac{\pi}{2}\ln 2$ by self-similarity), we obtain

$$\int_0^{\pi/2}\frac{t\cos t}{\sin t}\,dt=0-\left(-\frac{\pi}{2}\ln 2\right)=\frac{\pi}{2}\ln 2.$$

**Conclusion.**

$$I=2\cdot\frac{\pi}{2}\ln 2=\pi\ln 2.$$

$$\boxed{\displaystyle\int_0^{\infty}\frac{\arctan^2(x)}{x^2}\,dx=\pi\ln 2.}$$

